{"number": 31, "text": "                            ex'treme            and      ex'tremity\n                             Suffixes         and      word        stress       (3)\n\n                            Some   words  don’t change their stress pattern when a  suffix is added to the root word, but do\n                            change  the pronunciation  of the vowel  in the main stressed syllable. Compare:\n                                   ex'treme  — ex'tremity       de'rive — de'rivative\n\n                            The  following  table shows a number   of possible vowel changes.  The  main  stressed syllable and\n                            the pronunciation   of the vowel in this syllable are shown:\n                              =>                              => /e/                            =  |e/\n                                  ‘bible - ‘biblical /1/          ‘nation -         /z/             com'pete - com'petitive /e/\n                                  type -        /1/               de'fame - de'famatory /z/     /i:/ inter'vene - inter'vention /e/\n                                  wise -         /1/          le1/ sane -                       li:/ ob'scene - ob'scenity /e/\n                              =                               /ea/ com'pare - com'parative     | = /a/\n                                  ex'plore - ex'ploratory /p/ | /ea/ de'clare - de'clarative /ze/ /u:/ as'sume - as'sumption /a/\n                                  know  -            /D/      /a:/       - 'dramatise           /u:/ pre'sume - pre'sumption /a/\n\n\n                            In some  words,  as well as a change in the pronunciation  of the vowel  in the stressed syllable,\n                            there is also a change in the pronunciation  of the consonant(s) that follow  it.\n                                        ig'nite — ig'nition                /u:s/ pro'duce  — pro'duction,  pro'ductive /ak/\n                                   /ain/ sign —            /1gn/           /u:s/ intro'duce — intro'duction, intro'ductory /ak/\n\n                          © In other words   like this, there is a change in the pronunciation of the vowel in the stressed\n                            syllable and also the spelling of either this vowel and/or the consonant(s) that follow it:\n                                  col'lide — col'lision /13/                             de'ceive — de'ception, de'ceptive /ep/\n                            /aid/ di'vide — di'vision /13/                              re'peat — re'petitive /e/\n                            /aid/ pro'vide — pro'vision /13/                        /a:/ ex'ample —  ex'emplary  /e/\n                                  de'scribe — de'scription, de'scriptive                re'tain — re'tention /e/\n                            /atb/ pre'scribe — pre'scription, pre'scriptive /1p/        ex'plain — ex'planatory  /e/\n                            /atb/ sub'scribe — sub'scription /1p/                   /ai/ ap'ply — ap'plicable /1/\n\n                       How  do you spell\n\n                                                  ext\n\n\n                            Words   that do change  their stress pattern when a suffix is added to the root also commonly\n                            change  their pronunciation  in one or more  syllable:\n                                   pro'nounce   — pronunci'ation             pre'fer — 'preferable\n                                     /al\n\n          2                 There  are many  words  like this, and a great variety of pronunciation changes.  For these words  it\n                            is best to check pronunciation  in a dictionary such as CALD    or CEPD   (see Section E5 Further\n                            readin\n                                   g).\n          Language\n\n\n                  32           English Pronunciation in Use (Advanced)", "char_count": 3847, "mean_conf": 80.96, "word_count": 418, "words": [{"text":